The built-in terraform-fmt formatter knows how to format terraform and HCL code.
If you aren't familiar with basic codefmt usage yet, see main.vroom first.

We'll set up codefmt and configure the vroom environment, then jump into some
examples.

  :source $VROOMDIR/setupvroom.vim

  :call codefmt#SetWhetherToPerformIsAvailableChecksForTesting(0)


The terraform-fmt formatter expects the terraform executable to be installed on
your system.

  % resource "google_compute_instance" "default" {<CR>
  |name = "test"<CR>
  |machine_type = "n1-standard-1" }
  :FormatCode terraform-fmt
  ! terraform fmt -.*
  $ resource "google_compute_instance" "default" {
  $   name         = "test"
  $   machine_type = "n1-standard-1"
  $ }
  resource "google_compute_instance" "default" {
    name         = "test"
    machine_type = "n1-standard-1"
  }

The name or path of the terraform executable can be configured via the
terraform_executable flag if the default of "terraform" doesn't work.

  :Glaive codefmt terraform_executable='myterraform'
  :FormatCode terraform-fmt
  ! myterraform fmt -.*
  $ f()
  f()
  :Glaive codefmt terraform_executable='terraform'


The terraform filetype will use the terraform-fmt formatter by default.

  @clear
  % resource "google_compute_instance" "default" { }
  :set filetype=terraform
  :FormatCode
  ! terraform fmt -.*
  $ resource "google_compute_instance" "default" {}
  resource "google_compute_instance" "default" {}

The hcl filetype will also use the terraform-fmt formatter by default.

  @clear
  % key = "value"
  :set filetype=hcl
  :FormatCode
  ! terraform fmt -.*
  $ key = "value"
  key = "value"

  :set filetype=

It can format specific line ranges of code using :FormatLines.

  @clear
  % resource "google_compute_instance" "default" {<CR>
  |name = "test"<CR>
  |machine_type = "n1-standard-1" }<CR>
  |resource "google_compute_instance" "new-and-improved" {<CR>
  |name = "other"<CR>
  |machine_type = "n1-standard-1" }
  :4,6FormatLines terraform-fmt
  ! terraform fmt -.*
  $ resource "google_compute_instance" "new-and-improved" {
  $   name         = "other"
  $   machine_type = "n1-standard-1"
  $ }
